course: dictionary
lesson: 10220
title: Short forms and what they stand for
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: TLC is an abbreviation, which is a short way of writing a longer phrase. Each letter stands for a word: T is for tender, L is for loving, and C is for care. It is usually written in capitals, so you can spot it quickly.
explain: «The old bike needs some TLC.» Nobody means the bike needs three letters. The clue is needs: the bike is worn out and needs someone to look after it kindly. So TLC means tender loving care, which is gentle attention for someone or something that needs it.
explain: Read the whole sentence to check. «The puppy was shivering in the rain, so Mum gave it some TLC.» Shivering and rain are clues that the puppy needed care. Now swap in the full words: "gave it some tender loving care" still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: TLC is an abbreviation, a short form of tender loving care.
fact: Tender means gentle, loving means warm and caring, and care means looking after someone or something.
fact: TLC is usually written in capitals.
fact: «Needs some TLC» can describe a sick child, a pet, or an old bike that needs looking after.
fact: In adverts, «needs some TLC» can mean a place is in bad condition and needs repairs.
exercise: What do the letters TLC stand for? | Tiny, lazy cats | Time, lots, cake | Talk, laugh, call | *Tender loving care
exercise: «The puppy was shivering, so Mum gave it some TLC.» What does TLC mean here? | A loud telling-off | *Kind, gentle care | A new toy | A trip to the vet's
exercise: An advert says «Small flat, needs some TLC.» What does the flat need? | A new coat of pink paint | *Repairs, because it is in bad condition | Lots more rooms | A bigger garden
test: «My gran was poorly, so we gave her plenty of TLC.» What does TLC suggest? | *Kind, gentle care | A big argument | Sending her away to school | Loud music and a party
test: Which way is TLC correctly written? | tlc | tLc | Tlc | *TLC
exam: «The broken chair needs some TLC.» What is the best meaning of TLC here? | Needs a bigger seat | Needs a coat of paint only | *Needs gentle care and repairs | Needs to be thrown away at once
